$DIR/test.p:13:50: string(b) of a byte slice is not permitted in Plutonium (it would replace bytes that are not UTF-8; use strings.FromUTF8 or strings.FromUTF8Lossy)
    Plutonium has no string(b) for a byte slice b. Every string holds valid
    UTF-8 text, but a slice of bytes can hold anything. In Go the conversion
    keeps the bytes. Here it would have to change each byte that is not valid
    UTF-8 into the replacement character U+FFFD without telling you, so two
    different slices, such as {0xfe} and {0xff}, would give equal strings.
    Say what you mean. strings.FromUTF8(b) returns the string and an error, and
    fails if the bytes are not valid UTF-8. strings.FromUTF8Lossy(b) returns a
    string in which each invalid byte is replaced by U+FFFD. If the bytes are
    binary data, such as a digest used as a map key, keep them in a byte array
    or a slice and not in a string.
    Go strings may hold any bytes. Plutonium strings are text, so a conversion
    from bytes must either check them or replace some of them, and it must not
    do the second without the program saying so. The data in a string must not
    change silently.
    To fail on bytes that are not UTF-8, return the error:
        import "pluto/strings"

        s, err := strings.FromUTF8(b)
        if err != nil {
            return "", err
        }
        return s, nil
    To replace each byte that is not valid UTF-8 with U+FFFD, as string(b) did:
        import "pluto/strings"

        return strings.FromUTF8Lossy(b)
    (see "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24)
    (run "pluto explain E2015" for a longer explanation)
